#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccfca315-de53-561a-9ae1-18a2124ea24b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5c02a325-ceed-525b-b0af-dcc7d082c777" :question/math-academy-id "e-10809" :question/problem "For the function $y = f(x)$ shown below, what is the domain of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$\n\n![](images/ad/adf44ae3f3c8cb3bbe1958cca12e559b68d4d95247d087c69cb90425dbc05785.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq f(x) \\lt 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4acd94-b4a7-59ce-b6cb-3d3310b167fd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of an Inverse Function Using a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "3d784815-6930-5b44-b1a4-6cf2d3e2eeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10810" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $-3\\lt x \\leq 3$ and the range of $f$ is $-2\\leq f(x) \\lt 2.$ What is the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt  f^{-1}(x) \\leq 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9429da-a9d4-5dfc-940a-ef24066a5b65" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "93801dca-c5a6-5c7e-b10b-1e407332927a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10823" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c187a2fdc4c34135836afd2f28af17776cee6408b1e7bf3124d4833ca0529000.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x)$ shown above, what is the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\lt x \\lt 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\lt f^{-1}(x) \\lt 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4acd94-b4a7-59ce-b6cb-3d3310b167fd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of an Inverse Function Using a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560455} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560504} {:answer/id #uuid "70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560604} {:answer/id #uuid "e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560807} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :ans